## 1. BLOCKING — the probe is owned, with a narrow rule

`scripts/probe-builder-runtime.mjs:8-33` compares the builder cell's whole
environment against a hard-coded copy of W-125's 18 names, and it runs **first**
in that cell, before the builder command. Behaviour 2 adds a 19th name whenever a
browser cache resolves, so with revision 4's text the probe throws
`environment allowlist mismatch` and every real `bisellium run` with a cache dies
as `confined builder/probe exited 1` — the producer's own unsandboxed acceptance
run included, since the sentinel makes the cache always resolve there.

Reproduced here before ruling, `env -i` with the exact name sets:

```
18 names                                        -> clears the check, dies later at "unexpected cwd"
18 + PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=/browsers          -> environment allowlist mismatch
18 + PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=<host cache path>   -> environment allowlist mismatch
probe `expected` list  ==  builderCell(...).env's 18 names   -> true, as a set
```

**Ruling: the probe joins *Files owned*, for that comparison alone.** The reason
in one paragraph: W-130 already owns the 19-name contract — *Interfaces* calls it
"W-130's one deliberate extension of that contract", behaviour 2(d) asserts it on
the descriptor, and `run.test.ts:670` reads it from inside the cell — and the
probe is that contract's *enforcer*, compiled into the cell as a second hard-coded
copy of the same list. An opus that owns a contract and not its enforcer does not
have a scope boundary, it has a contradiction, and `filesOwned` (`:428-431`) is
what actually authorizes an export, so the ruling has to be in the list rather
than in prose. The builder's second option — keep the name out of the cell
environment and inject it only for the post-probe command — is rejected on two
grounds: the probe spawns that command with `env: process.env`, so it needs the
same edit anyway and nothing is saved; and the cell would then assert a 19-name
contract it does not carry, which contradicts behaviour 2(d), `run.test.ts:670`
and the entire sentinel apparatus MUST B installed in revision 2. The edit is
bounded by a rule, not by trust: exactly W-125's 18, plus
`PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` **only when its value is exactly `/browsers`**, so the
19th name cannot carry a host path into the cell and the probe stays fail-closed
against every other name. *Files owned*, *Interfaces* (the 19-name paragraph),
behaviour 2 and *Out of scope* now agree, and *Out of scope* keeps `requireBrowser`
and the `--require-browser` browser hunt excluded by name so the narrowing cannot
be read as the whole file.

**Tests that pin the new rule: behaviour 2(g) and 2(h).** 2(g) spawns the probe as
a child with a constructed environment and reads its output for the mismatch
message: absent for the 18, absent for the 18 plus `/browsers`, **present** for any
other value of that name and for an unrelated 19th name — four assertions, four
messages, so the rule cannot be half-built into "any 19th name passes". 2(h)
asserts the probe's `expected` literal and `builderCell`'s env agree as a set,
because two hand-maintained copies of one allowlist is precisely what produced this
stop. Both rows are socket-free, bwrap-free and browser-free, like every other red
in this brief.

**No new red, and none is authorized.** These are lettered rows inside behaviour 2,
whose red is already recorded assertion-level at its own committed tree; they add
no behaviour, the count stays at five, and `# behaviour: 2` still names a test
whose first failing assertion is 2(a). Re-recording it so the log mentions the
probe would be backfilling evidence for a fix-forward of my own contradiction —
the same reasoning that left behaviour 4's red untouched in revision 4. The rows
must still be written and seen failing before the probe is edited; the discipline
holds even where the recorded artefact does not move.

## 2. `node:os` was a text error in *Files owned*

`host-cells.mjs` imports `node:path` and `node:fs` — `statSync`/`realpathSync`,
read-only, which *Interfaces* specifies and the implementation does. `node:os` is
not imported at all: `homedir()` is the runner's call, which is what the module's
freedom from ambient state rests on, and *Interfaces* says so. The builder
followed the correct half. *Files owned* is corrected to name `node:fs` and to
state that `node:os` is deliberately absent.

## 3. A prettier-only reformat after the red is permitted

Yes, under two conditions. A red replays at the tree its own log names —
behaviour 4's is pinned to `tree:b4ed23db65198744a5f7d3fc0be85a93b92121b0` — so no
later commit can reach the bytes the replay executes, and `format:check` is
required green at the final source tree. The conditions: `npx prettier --write` on
that path alone, in its own commit, with `git diff -w` empty against the
pre-reformat tree, which is the mechanical proof that no token, assertion, message
or canned transcript moved. A substantive edit under cover of formatting is a
stop, and the red is not re-recorded. "Unchanged, and already red" in this brief
means the behaviour's number, file, selector and assertions — not its whitespace;
the brief now says so where it makes the claim.

## 4. The derived harness is what satisfies the measurement bullet

The builder is right that `measure-replay-acceptance-2.mjs` cannot meet the bullet
alone: it carries its own embedded ladder and never loads
`scripts/replay-accept.mjs`, so it can report `22 transcripts, 0 disagreements`
while the module disagrees with it. Revision 4's wording implied one run where two
are needed, and that is my defect.

`~/.bisellium-evidence/w130-measure-impl.mjs` satisfies it, and better than a
hand-written harness would: it was **mechanically derived** from the retained kit
by `w130-measure-impl-derive.mjs`, three `once()` substitutions that throw unless
each pattern is unique, so the kit's ladder survives as `scriptClassify` verbatim
and the comparison is `accepted` **and** `reason` equal, per transcript, across all
twenty-two. Both logs show `22 transcripts, 0 disagreements`, with the kit's own
unmodified run retained beside them. *Acceptance* is amended to require both runs,
to name the derivation requirement (derived, never hand-copied), and to retain the
derivation script, the derived harness and both outputs together under
`~/.bisellium-evidence/W-130-spec/`. A disagreement remains a stop; neither the kit
nor the derivation may be edited to produce agreement.

